in the high-profile trial of theranos  founder elizabeth holmes prosecutors  have been trying to prove that holmes  intentionally defrauded patients and  investors by making false claims about  her company's blood testing technology  holmes has pleaded not guilty this  company is about being able to change  the health care industry as prosecutors  continue to make their case speculation  has grown about whether holmes will take  the stand all these witnesses we've  heard from have said  how they were totally enamored by her  they totally believed her she had a  presence that controlled her room to  catch you up on three key pieces of  evidence presented at trial so far and a  look at what's to come we talked to wall  street journal reporter sarah randazzo  who's been covering the trial in  california  first audio recordings have given jurors  the chance to hear holmes pitch theranos  to investors in 2013. the ability to  take a technology like this and put it  in flight specifically on a medevac has  the potential to change survival rates  we're hearing elizabeth holmes touting  how they can do all of these things with  the machines how they're working with  the military and the military is huge  for them and so all of these things that  at this point the jurors have heard uh  contained a lot of falsehoods and  weren't quite true and so now they're  hearing elizabeth holmes say it directly  which is pretty powerful some investors  said holmes's promise to help members of  the military compel them to invest in  the company in cross-examination  attorneys for homes have made the case  that theranos investors should have  asked questions if they had concerns and  that they knew investing in a new type  of technology like the kind theranos was  developing came with risks prosecutors  also read private text messages between  holmes and her former boyfriend sonny  balwani who is the number two at the  company there's a lot of texts showing  sunny and elizabeth texting about  concerns or things that they're worried  about in one 2015 message following  media appearances made by holmes balwani  wrote fyi i am worried about  overexposure without solid substance  which is lacking right now holmes  responded that the media coverage helped  theranos get a business deal it's  important because a big part of this  trial is showing elizabeth holmes's  intent and that she intended to deceive  so if jurors can come to believe that  she thought there were a bunch of  concerns but then publicly was saying  nothing was wrong  that can help them in their  deliberations the defense has argued  that holmes relied on balwani for  accurate information about the company  and that she believed she was telling  the truth about theranos bawani who  faces his own separate trial has denied  any wrongdoing  another key piece of evidence shown by  the prosecution an allegedly forged  endorsement this document emblazoned  with the pfizer logo purported to show  that theranos's technology had been  validated by the pharmaceutical giant  but prosecutors say it was used to  deceive investors what's come out is  that they did work with pfizer in the  very early years of the company pfizer  then had a scientist shane webber  analyze everything that they'd seen and  internally write a report that concluded  they didn't want to pursue the  relationship weber said he himself told  holmes that pfizer wasn't interested in  working with theranos but about a year  later theranos put out a report with the  pfizer logo on it which holmes used to  pitch to investors some investors  testified that they took the report at  face value and that it played into their  decision to invest many millions of  dollars of course there's been plenty of  other evidence presented at trial so far  which you can read more about at wsj.com  so what's next in the closely watched  trial the defense will soon have a  chance to make its case and it could go  a few different ways as lawyers  suggested in a court filing they could  argue that holmes was in an abusive  relationship with balwani that left her  under his control a lawyer for balwani  disputed any allegations of abuse or as  the defense argued in opening statements  it could continue making the case that  holmes simply had a startup that failed  like many others in silicon valley the  defense could also simply rest hoping  that prosecutors didn't prove holmes's  guilt beyond a reasonable doubt i think  it's going to be a bit of a  surprise you know and shocked moment in  court when they stand up and say whether  they're calling someone or just you know  resting the case holmes is facing 12  counts of wire fraud and conspiracy to  commit wire fraud with the top charges  carrying a maximum federal prison  sentence of 20 years
 

